<LessonShell chapterNumber={3} lessonNumber={7}>

## No New Syntax

This lesson teaches you nothing you can do that you could not do yesterday. Every idea in it is about making the code you already write possible to come back to.

That sounds like a lesson to skim. It is the opposite. The gap between programmers who can maintain their own work and programmers who quietly rewrite it every few months is mostly made of the habits below, and they are cheap to acquire now and expensive to retrofit later.

## Magic Numbers

Read this program before you run it, and answer two questions: what does `0.15` mean, and do the two of them mean the same thing?

<PythonEnvironment config={magicConfig} />

You cannot tell. One is a service charge and one is a tip, and the fact that they are both fifteen percent today is a coincidence — a coincidence that will be silently destroyed the first time somebody changes "the rate" and changes both.

A bare number sitting in the middle of a program like that is called a **magic number**: magic because it works, and nobody knows why.

## Constants

The fix is to give the number a name:

<PythonEnvironment config={namedConfig} />

Two things improved, and the second is the one that matters:

1. **It says what it means.** `bill * SERVICE_RATE` needs no comment.
2. **The two rates are now separate things that happen to be equal.** Change `TIP_RATE` to `0.10` and the service charge is untouched. In the first version there was no way to express that intention at all.

A **constant** is a variable you do not intend to change once it is set. By convention it is named in `UPPER_CASE_WITH_UNDERSCORES`, and that capitalisation is the entire mechanism.

> **Python does not enforce constants.** There is no keyword for one, and nothing stops you assigning to `SERVICE_RATE` later — the interpreter will not even blink. The capitals are a message to human readers: *this is set once at the top and not touched again.* It works because programmers honour it, which is a weaker guarantee than a compiler and a surprisingly effective one.

Put your constants at the top of the program, above the code that uses them. That is where readers look for them, and it means the things most likely to need changing are the things easiest to find.

**When is a number worth naming?** A useful test: if it appears twice, or if a reader would have to stop and work out what it is, name it. `total // 100` to convert pence to pounds needs no constant — everybody knows what 100 pence is. `bill * 0.15` does.

## The Conventions Python Programmers Share

Python has a style guide, **PEP 8**, and the whole language community broadly follows it. That shared style is worth more than any individual rule in it: code written by strangers looks like code written by you.

The parts that matter at this stage:

- **`snake_case` for variables**, as Lesson 3.1 said. `crew_count`, not `crewCount` or `CrewCount`.
- **`UPPER_CASE` for constants.** `MAX_SPEED`, `VAT_PERCENT`.
- **Spaces around operators.** `total = price * quantity`, not `total=price*quantity`. It reads as words rather than as a barcode.
- **No space just inside brackets.** `print(name)`, not `print( name )`.
- **A space after each comma.** `print(a, b)`, not `print(a,b)`.
- **Blank lines to group related lines** into paragraphs. Programs, like prose, are easier to read in chunks than as a wall.
- **Keep lines reasonably short.** PEP 8 says 79 characters; the spirit is that a reader should not have to scroll sideways.

> **One place this site differs from PEP 8, on purpose.** All the code here is indented with **two spaces**, because the editors on these pages are narrow and deeply nested code would run off the side. Real PEP 8 says **four**, and that is what you will see in almost every Python project you meet outside this curriculum. Neither is wrong and the difference is cosmetic — but be aware of it, and when you write Python elsewhere, use four. The rule that actually matters is that a file is consistent with itself.

## Comments Say Why

You have used comments since Karel, so this is short. What changes now is the standard you hold them to.

A comment that restates the code is noise:

```python
count = count + 1   # add one to count
```

A comment that explains **why** is worth writing:

```python
count = count + 1   # the sentinel itself is not crew
```

The code can always tell a reader *what* happens — that is what code is. What it can never tell them is what you were thinking, which alternative you rejected, or which apparently-arbitrary number came from somebody else's specification. Write those down.

Two more habits:

- **A wrong comment is worse than no comment.** When you change code, change the comment above it. A stale comment actively misleads, and readers trust it right up until it costs them an afternoon.
- **If a line needs a paragraph of explanation, consider whether better names would do the job instead.** `if not is_valid:` needs no comment. `if not v:` needs one, and would rather have been renamed.

## Readable Beats Clever

The last habit is an attitude. There is nearly always a shorter way to write any piece of code, and it is nearly always worse.

You will not be judged on how few lines you used. You — specifically you, in six months, having forgotten all of this — will be the person who has to read it. Write for that person.

